  "item": "mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_left]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "TermLike requires this callback on the unit-test-only recorder.\n The authoring renderer rewrites whole lines rather than moving\n horizontally; no application input can select this no-op callback.\n Real terminal output remains covered by the authoring PTY tests."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/authoring/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_left]` is valid.\n\n- **Where it sits:** The citation is a fenced `{cite}` block placed directly on an explicit function with a body, which is the form the built-in allows for a function-level coverage exception.\n- **Substantive reason:** The commentary gives a real justification. `indicatif::TermLike` requires the callback. `RecordingTerm` is a `#[cfg(test)]` adapter that only records whole lines. The renderer redraws whole lines by moving up, clearing and writing, and does not move horizontally. That is why the no-op can't be reached.\n- **Agreement with the source:** The function body is `Ok(())`. The sibling `write_line`, `write_str`, `move_cursor_up` and `clear_line` methods are the ones the tests rely on. In `BatchProgress`, the renderer is driven only through `set_position`, `set_message`, `force_draw`, `println`, `finish` and `abandon`. Nothing in the code selects a horizontal-move callback. I could not open the indicatif source to confirm that its draw path never calls `move_cursor_left`. I accepted that part from the BatchProgress code and the commentary.\n- **Real-terminal coverage:** The claim that real terminal output is covered by PTY tests holds. `tests/authoring_batch.rs` has `run_in_pty` and `run_in_pty_with_args`, and the test `empty_batches_and_early_errors_clear_the_terminal_display` uses them. `BatchProgress::new` wires `console::Term::buffered_stderr()` as the real draw target.\n\nThe citation is not redundant, because the function carries no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference.",
